From 0995c175287bda62d6c214cc25547d0e1e9cd5d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adeesababa <263047020+adeesababa@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 22:52:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] NVR extension 1.7-displace: tessellation displacement for
 registered D3D9 shader pairs

A separate interface, ID3D9NvrExtTess (d3d9_nvr_ext_api.h; ID3D9NvrExt and its
version are unchanged). NVR registers a vertex shader with a tessellation control
shader and pixel shaders of the same family with tessellation evaluation shaders,
before the D3D9 shaders are created. A draw whose bound vertex and pixel shaders
belong to one family then runs VS -> TCS -> TES -> PS from a patch list of 3
control points (D3D9DeviceEx::ApplyPrimitiveType hook): a triangle list as is, a
triangle strip from DrawPrimitive / DrawIndexedPrimitive as three patch lists over
its index windows from its first, second and third index (every strip triangle
once; the TCS gets the window in its push data and swaps the odd triangles' first
two control points). Every other draw takes the normal path. The TES may read the
pixel shader's textures and samplers, the vertex shader's float constants and the
clip planes; per-draw push data gives it the compacted constant indices and the
signs of two pixel shader registers. The interfaces (TCS inputs vs VS outputs,
TES outputs vs the PS inputs the VS writes) are checked on the CS thread at a
pair's first draw; a mismatch is logged and the pair draws untessellated.
Wireframe debug view. The TCS and TES push blocks start at shader offset 32 (MaxSharedPushDataSize),
after D3D9's shared block, as DXVK's own D3D9 shaders do. Pixel shader texture binds also mark the TES stage once
anything is registered (pipeline-library layouts keep the TES descriptors in the
pre-raster set).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
